  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Gage Jump shut down the Angels over seven scoreless innings, and the game was decided in the first inning.",
    "what_this_means": "Jump's shutout gave Sacramento room to breathe, but it was the early offensive eruption that made the window permanent. Five runs in the first turned this into a blowout by the time the lineups settled. Shea Langeliers drove in three of them with his home run. The bullpen finished what Jump started. Mason Barnett and Hogan Harris combined for two scoreless innings with four strikeouts total. The game was never in doubt after the first thirty minutes, and that's the only real story here. A dominant starter and a dead Angels lineup added up to one thing: control. The offense converted two of seven RISP situations into actual runs, which is enough when the pitcher doesn't give anything back. This is what stabilizing looks like after a two-game skid. Not polished, just functional. The trend improved because the formula held when it mattered, and the division doesn't care how it looks.",
    "what_to_watch": "Jos\u00e9 Soriano starts for the Angels tonight with a 2.79 ERA, and Sacramento sits 1.5 back in the AL West where every single game counts in a crowded stretch.",
